{% doc %}
  Motion gate — arms a section's entrance before the section paints.

  Every sv- section that reveals on scroll holds its content at opacity 0
  under a `.sv-motion-ready` class, and adds that class from script so
  that a visitor whose script never runs is left with a complete, fully
  visible page rather than a blank one. That contract is right; where it
  went wrong was the placement.

  The script that added the class sat at the FOOT of the section, after
  all the markup. On the FAQ that is seventy kilobytes of questions — far
  more than the browser will hold before its first paint. So the real
  sequence a visitor saw was:

      paint the whole page, visible
      reach the script, add the class, everything drops to opacity 0
      observer fires, fade it back in

  which reads exactly like a page that failed to load and then recovered.

  Rendered as the first child of the section, this closes that window:
  the class is on the root before any of its children have been parsed,
  so nothing is ever painted in a state it is about to be pulled out of.
  The entrance then plays from a clean start, which is what it was
  written to do.

  The guards mirror the ones the section's own script uses, and must
  keep mirroring them — arming the hold when nothing will release it is
  the one failure mode worse than the flash. No IntersectionObserver
  means nothing would ever add `.sv-vis`; reduced motion means the
  section wants no entrance at all.

  Uses parentElement rather than a selector so it carries no knowledge
  of the section it sits in: drop it in as the first child and it gates
  that element, whatever it is called.
{% enddoc %}

<script>
  (function () {
    var el = document.currentScript && document.currentScript.parentElement;
    if (!el) return;
    if (!('IntersectionObserver' in window)) return;
    if (window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) return;
    el.classList.add('sv-motion-ready');
  })();
</script>
